DENSITY PIPELINE EXTENSION 
Hours Worked: 13 hours

Some components were completed (but not fully implemented) on April 10th, the total no. of hours worked take that into consideration. 

Purpose
Extension of the MW–M31 density analysis pipeline to include full multi-epoch structural diagnostics, 2D projections, mixing analysis, and analytic model fitting (NFW / Einasto / Hernquist). This builds on the existing kinematics and density framework to enable full morphological and structural interpretation of the merger evolution.


Work Summary
- Extended 2D surface density pipeline Σ(x,y) for MW, M31, and total system
- Implemented log-scaled visualization (log10 Σ) with Gaussian smoothing to preserve dynamic range across core and tidal halo structures
- Developed radial density comparison framework ρ_MW(r) vs ρ_M31 across multiple epochs
- Derived mass-mixing metric f_mix(r,t) to quantify halo interpenetration and mixing front evolution
- Built residual analysis module comparing simulated density profiles to NFW model to identify spatial–temporal deviations during merger evolution
- Integrated analytic density model fitting (NFW, Einasto, Hernquist) using log-space optimization for numerical stability
- Extracted time-dependent structural parameters (e.g., r_s, Einasto α) and computed reduced χ² for model comparison across snapshots
- Generated multi-panel figures and animations summarizing density evolution, mixing structure, and model performance across ~800 snapshots

Notes / Challenges
- Maintaining consistent normalization and scaling across epochs for physical comparability
- Handling extreme dynamic range in density fields (multiple orders of magnitude)
- Ensuring numerical stability in log-space fitting and residual calculations
- High computational cost due to repeated model fitting across large snapshot set
